// @check
// @error-at 22:22 unknown type 'Bogus' for field 'f'
// @error-at 26:12 Type `Dee` is defined in terms of itself with no base type, so it can never have a value
// @error-at 27:18 Type `Eee` is `never` (an empty type — `Obj & "lit"`, an object and a string literal, have no common values), so it has no values
// THE SOUNDNESS BOUNDARY, IN THE DIRECTION THAT MUST NOT MOVE. Resolving a forward
// reference on demand makes a declaration's body available earlier; it must not make a
// BAD declaration acceptable, and it must not report a bad one TWICE — the on-demand
// caller and the source-order driver both reach every declaration, and only the first
// may resolve it.
//
// Each of the three below is a genuine error whose declaration is FORWARD-referenced
// from a struct field, so each arrives through the on-demand path rather than the
// driver's own walk:
//
//   `Bogus`  — a name no declaration provides.        (unknown type, at the FIELD)
//   `Dee`    — a pure self-cycle, no base case.       (at the DECLARATION, once)
//   `Eee`    — a disjoint intersection, empty.        (at the DECLARATION, once)
//
// EXACTLY ONE diagnostic each, and the two `never` ones land on the DECLARATION rather
// than on the use site — the property `self-alias-still-clean.vl` and
// `never-value-self-intersection.vl` pin for the back-declared spelling, held here for
// the forward-declared one.
type HasBogus = { f: Bogus }
type HasDee = { f: Dee }
type HasEee = { f: Eee }

type Dee = Dee
type Eee = Obj & "lit"
type Obj = { a: string }
